modern_name":"Benin","min_lon":0.652206728684462,"min_lat":6.179760538057131,"max_lon":2.422162731198608,"max_lat":8.791120803356126,"source_file":"world_1900.geojson","source_row":null}],"identities":[{"qid":"Q468814","from_t":1783,"to_t":1894.0383561643835,"method":"name-label+date","confidence":0.95,"label":"Dahomey","description":"1600–1904 kingdom in West Africa","inception":1600,"dissolved":1894.0383561643835,"enwiki":"Dahomey","summary":"The Kingdom of Dahomey (; Fon: Danhɔmɛ, lit. 'In the Belly of  Chief Dan') was a West African kingdom located within the present-day Republic of Benin that existed from approximately 1600 until 1904. It developed on the Abomey Plateau among the Fon people in the early 17th century and became a regional power in the 18th century by expanding south to conquer key cities like Whydah belonging to the Kingdom of Whydah on the Atlantic coast, which granted it unhindered access to the Atlantic Slave Trade.\nEnding tributary status to the Oyo Empire, Dahomey became a key regional state for much of the middle 19th century. European visitors extensively documented the kingdom, and it became one of the most familiar African nations known to Europeans."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q880285","value_label":"Gezo","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":"head of state","property":"P35","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Gezo"},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q189685","value_label":"Abomey","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Abomey"},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"language","value_qid":"Q33291","value_label":"Fon","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P2936","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Fon"},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"official_religion","value_qid":"Q716356","value_label":"Dahomean religion","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P3075","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Dahomean religion"},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"government","value_qid":"Q184558","value_label":"absolute monarchy","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P122","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"absolute monarchy"},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"predecessor","value_qid":"Q640010","value_label":"Great Ardra","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1365","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Great Ardra"},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"predecessor","value_qid":"Q1716449","value_label":"Kingdom of Whydah","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1365","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Kingdom of Whydah"},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"successor","value_qid":"Q797505","value_label":"French Dahomey","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1366","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"French Dahomey"},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"flag","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Dahomey flag 1889.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P41","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"flag","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Flag of Ghezo of Dahomey.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P41","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q468814","kind":"coat","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Coat of arms of Béhanzin.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P94","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null}],"people":{"Q880285":{"label":"Gezo","description":"king of Dahomey","enwiki":"Ghezo","summary":"Ghezo, also spelled Gezo, was King of Dahomey (present-day Republic of Benin) from 1818 until 1858. Ghezo replaced his brother Adandozan (who ruled from 1797 to 1818) as king through a coup with the assistance of the Brazilian slave trader Francisco Félix de Sousa. He ruled over the kingdom during a tumultuous period, punctuated by the British blockade of the ports of Dahomey in order to stop the Atlantic slave trade.\nGhezo ended Dahomey's tributary status to the Oyo Empire.","image":"Gezo (2).jpg","birth":1790,"death":1858}},"media":{"Dahomey flag 1889.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo